# The nftables side of the XDP comparison, published with the result so the
# comparison can be checked rather than trusted.
#
# It is deliberately the fair fight, not a straw man:
#
#  - the drop lives in the `raw` hook at PREROUTING priority, the earliest place
#    nftables can act, before conntrack and before the packet is committed to the
#    normal path. This is where a competent operator puts a volumetric drop, and
#    it is the closest nftables gets to what XDP does before the stack;
#  - the rule matches the same packets xdp_drop matches: UDP to the flood's
#    destination port. Nothing broader, nothing narrower;
#  - a `counter` is attached so the drop is measurable and cannot be accused of
#    silently passing traffic.
#
# What it does NOT do, on purpose: no hashlimit, no set-based source tracking, no
# SYN handling. Those are policies Carapace also implements and would move the
# comparison away from "the same drop, two datapaths" toward "two different
# policies". The point of the measurement is where the drop happens, not how
# clever it is.
#
# Load with:  nft -f compare.nft        Flush with:  nft delete table ip carapace_cmp

table ip carapace_cmp {
	chain drop_flood {
		type filter hook prerouting priority raw; policy accept;

		# The flood is 64-byte UDP to port 19000 (scripts/lab/gen-udp-flood.sh).
		# The legitimate probe traffic uses other ports and is untouched.
		udp dport 19000 counter drop
	}
}
